 **Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-*   **Pre-integrated Slabs:** Storing pre-integrated slab integrals leads to errors because the warping undoes the orthogonality of the rays. The integrals for warped rays would be looked up incorrectly. Therefore, only a cross-sectional texture of the radially symmetric RBF (Gaussian) is stored, which is then scaled by the orthogonal kernel value [S1].
-*   **Staircase Artifacts:** Since the method extracts kernel slices (point-sampling of the slice function) rather than pre-filtering slabs, staircase artifacts may occur. Pre-integrated rendering can be added using interpolated densities of front and back slices as indices to overcome this [S1].
-*   **GPU Hardware:** Direct analytical slicing of the general ellipsoid requires square roots and conditional statements, which are not well accelerated on GPU hardware. The warping method avoids these by using vector/matrix operations and texture mapping [S1].
